<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Silesia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper Silesia is the south-eastern part of Silesia, on the upper Oder, today mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> with a part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was divided among Piast duchies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Racibórz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>, which came under the Bohemian crown. Later it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and became a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and industrial region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-silesia.jpg" alt="Upper Silesia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daviidos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Market_Square_Opole_Southside_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
